<clickhouse>
    <!--
      ClickHouse closes an idle HTTP connection after keep_alive_timeout, which
      defaults to 10 seconds. Every client here is a pooled one, and a pool
      keeps connections far longer than that: HikariCP holds an idle connection
      for ten minutes by default and does not learn that the far end hung up.

      The result was a pool full of dead handles. Each borrow blocked for the
      full 30 second connection timeout and failed, and because Spring runs
      scheduled tasks on a single thread by default, one blocked borrow stalled
      every scheduled task in the service — the mirror funds and the arbitrage
      engine alike. The strategy service sat idle for 41 hours with every
      container reporting "Up".

      A minute gives the pool room to recycle connections on its own terms.
    -->
    <keep_alive_timeout>60</keep_alive_timeout>
</clickhouse>
